## Abstract

Current language models are progressing at an exponential pace, raising critical challenges regarding their alignment with human intentions. This article argues that contemporary methods (RLHF, behavioral guardrails) fundamentally fail to solve the alignment problem because of a structural limitation: they attempt to impose explicit constraints on statistical optimizers that lack implicit understanding. We introduce the *emergent alignment hypothesis* and propose an alternative paradigm based on cognitive development through immersion in a complex digital environment, formalizing a transition from objective optimization to experiential learning.

